WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:20.220
This is Sara Blakely and at age 27, she took
$5,000 dollars and turned it into more than

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.220
a billion.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:26.250
She did this by selling a new type of underwear
called Spanx, but her strategy was unlike

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.160
any other entrepreneur.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.790
Mark Zuckerberg owns 30% of Facebook.

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:37.510
Elon Musk owns just 20% of Tesla, but Sara
Blakely, she owns 100% of Spanx.

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.920
So what does Sara know that Elon and Zuck
don’t?

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.530
Most entrepreneurs in Silicon Valley raise
money at some point, and once they start raising

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.180
money, it becomes a self-perpetuating cycle.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:52.270
One round leads to the next, and pretty soon
even the best founders only own a fraction

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:53.390
of their business.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:57.890
But Sara discovered an alternative strategy
that every entrepreneur should pay attention

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:01.460
to, whether or not you plan on raising money
for your business.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:06.530
If you were to ask Sara how she became a billionaire
without ever selling a single share of her

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:09.350
company’s stock, she would probably give
you a simple answer like this:

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:10.350
“I don’t know if it was a super conscious
decision along the way, more than I just didn’t

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:11.350
really have the need to.

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:15.660
Spanx was profitable from the first month
I was in business, I’m of the belief system,

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.710
for me to start small, think big, and scale
fast...”

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:23.030
But clearly if it were that easy, everyone
would do it, so there has to be more going

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:24.030
on here.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:28.960
Sara often gets ignored by mainstream business
news precisely because she never raised venture

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:29.960
funding.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:33.600
Most reporters only write stories about companies
when they announce a big funding round or

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.450
go public, but Spanx has just been growing
quietly for two decades now.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:43.090
And it’s too bad more people don’t focus
on Sara’s story because her initial idea

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:48.729
for Spanx follows the exact same pattern that
so many Silicon Valley investors preach: “Solve

00:01:48.729 --> 00:01:50.740
a problem you yourself have.”

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:57.920
“I started Spanx as a frustrated consumer,
I simply wanted to wear white pants to a party

00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.929
and have nothing show underneath it.

00:01:59.929 --> 00:02:00.929
My first product was footless pantihose.

00:02:00.929 --> 00:02:05.220
I took a product that already existed and
made a modification, I cut the feet out of

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:06.220
pantihose.”

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:09.890
Solving a problem for yourself is core to
so many great startup ideas.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:14.370
In fact, “Make something people want”
is the motto of Y Combinator.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:17.850
But lots of entrepreneurs follow this advice
and don’t become billionaires.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:23.120
Sara clearly has a secret formula for success,
so I listened to everything she’s ever said

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:27.069
publicly and boiled down her growth strategy
to 3 key factors.

00:02:27.069 --> 00:02:28.970
Let’s go through them one at a time.

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:32.819
When most people first come up with a startup
idea, they think they’ve struck gold.

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:37.450
It’s common to think that the idea is the
hardest part, and once you have a good idea,

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:42.030
you need to protect it at all costs, but this
is actually a complete misconception.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:46.430
Execution is everything in entrepreneurship
and great ideas don’t matter at all if you

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:50.220
can’t make the idea a reality and then distribute
it effectively.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:54.849
When Sara first came up with the idea for
Spanx, she kept the idea a secret for a year

00:02:54.849 --> 00:02:57.829
and even patented the design before launching
the company.

00:02:57.829 --> 00:03:01.140
This flies in the face of most traditional
thinking in Silicon Valley.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:05.329
Any investor will tell you to go out and build
something to make sure there is a market before

00:03:05.329 --> 00:03:10.409
working on time consuming things like patents,
but Sara saw things very differently, and

00:03:10.409 --> 00:03:15.159
this is factor number one: Sara sees patents
as marketing.

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:19.689
Legally speaking, a patent is a government
license that gives the inventor exclusive

00:03:19.689 --> 00:03:21.640
rights to a design for 20 years.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:26.390
It’s generally a way to stop people from
copying you, but Sara has never sued anyone

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:31.670
for copying Spanx design, instead, she focused
on using the patent as a way to build trust

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:33.090
in her consumers mind.

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:38.930
“I knew that I was patenting this to mostly
to be able to show the value and use it as

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:39.930
a marketing tool.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:44.599
I was mostly trying to convey, this is new,
this is different”

00:03:44.599 --> 00:03:49.700
The U.S. Patent system has become massively
bloated with dozens of patents for slight

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:51.590
variations on the same idea.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:56.819
And there is now a huge industry of patent
trolls who’s only business is buying patents

00:03:56.819 --> 00:04:09.180
and then suing companies who might be infringing
even slightly.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:13.950
Sara recognized that, even though the patent
system wasn’t working as intended anymore,

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:16.200
it could still be used to her advantage.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:21.510
Spanx customers would be drawn in by the “patent-pending”
label and investigate the product more thoroughly,

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:23.150
which would lead to purchases.

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:28.170
At my last company, Soylent, we took the opposite
approach, sharing our formulas publicly and

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:30.040
“open-sourcing” our ingredients.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:32.000
But the overarching goal was actually the
same.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.460
We were doing this for marketing reasons.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:39.490
By branding ourselves as the one health food
company that was completely open about how

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:44.590
we made our products, we won over consumers
who were hesitant to try Soylent themselves.

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:50.370
The big takeaway here is that, as an entrepreneur,
you need to be flexible while assessing opportunities.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:54.699
Even though patents may not protect you fully,
the decision to patent could still have an

00:04:54.699 --> 00:04:56.050
impact on your business.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:05:00.120
The key is to understand that getting a patent
is not a significant milestone in building

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:05.150
your business, only making a great product
and growing your customer base is significant.

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:08.319
I can’t tell you how many venture capitalists
have told me funny stories about crazy inventors

00:05:08.319 --> 00:05:12.449
walking into their offices thinking that they
had a great business just because they got

00:05:12.449 --> 00:05:13.449
a patent.

00:05:13.449 --> 00:05:17.800
There is so much more to growing a business
than thinking of an idea and filing some paperwork

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:23.150
to the US patent office, but Sara understands
that completely, and that leads to her second

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:24.590
key success factor.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:28.250
Once you’ve actually developed a product,
you need to be able to sell it, and Sara is

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:30.110
a master of selling products.

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:32.800
This makes sense, because she has a background
in sales.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.020
Before she started Spanx, she went door to
door selling fax machines and she learned

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:39.259
a ton of valuable skills during that time.

00:05:39.259 --> 00:05:43.180
It’s really common for entrepreneurs to
forget about the importance of sales and marketing

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:45.310
when they come from technology backgrounds.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:47.720
This was certainly the case for me when I
first moved to Silicon Valley.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:52.300
I thought that marketing was unnecessary if
you had a good product and generally thought

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:54.090
salespeople were just paid to socialize.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:58.210
But the best salespeople are actually masters
of human psychology.

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:02.440
The thing about psychology is that it can
be very counterintuitive at times.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:06.370
For example, most entrepreneurs think that
the best strategy is always to make a better

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:10.229
product and sell it at a lower price, but
that’s not always the case.

00:06:10.229 --> 00:06:15.099
See, back when Sara was selling fax machines
door to door, she wasn’t working for Canon,

00:06:15.099 --> 00:06:17.240
which was the most popular brand at the time.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:20.800
She was selling a cheaper machine called a
Dex, and customers weren’t really giving

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:21.919
her the time of day.

00:06:21.919 --> 00:06:25.660
But then she discovered something incredible
about the way people think about price and

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:26.660
quality:

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:31.889
“So then I made the price of my Dex slightly
higher than the canon and everybody stopped

00:06:31.889 --> 00:06:37.990
in my meetings and started asking more questions
and saying “Why is this Dex more money than

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:42.750
the canon what am I missing” and it gave
me the opportunity to really sell the features

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:46.340
and what was so special about the Dex fax
machine.

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:47.340
And people started buying”

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:50.300
This is the type of thing you would never
learn if you were entirely focused on your

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:51.300
product.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:55.020
At a certain point, you have to go and interact
with customers in order to see how they actually

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:56.020
behave.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:57.030
It might surprise you!

00:06:57.030 --> 00:07:01.169
Sara used everything she learned selling door
to door to grow Spanx at the early stage.

00:07:01.169 --> 00:07:02.169
And it worked!

00:07:02.169 --> 00:07:06.370
Lots of entrepreneurs confuse sales with advertising,
but they are actually two completely different

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:07.370
things.

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:11.690
Spanx didn’t advertise, at all, for years,
but that doesn’t mean Sara wasn’t laser

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:13.129
focused on sales.

00:07:13.129 --> 00:07:18.310
She called department stores and pitched directly
to buyers, never taking no for an answer.

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:22.320
Her product was solid, but it was really her
ability to convince retailers to take a chance

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:25.230
on Spanx that set her apart from the competition.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:29.729
Today, it’s common for startups to raise
money and instantly start spending that cash

00:07:29.729 --> 00:07:32.409
on facebook ads, but that can be a big mistake.

00:07:32.409 --> 00:07:36.699
Instead of seeing paid advertising as the
only route to growth, try and broaden your

00:07:36.699 --> 00:07:40.410
horizons and think of all the ways you could
reach your potential customer.

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:44.440
The more you do as an individual entrepreneur,
the more you’ll learn about your customer

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:47.650
and be able to tailor your sales pitch to
fit your specific market.

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:51.599
Sara’s determination didn’t stop once
she got a yes from a retailer though.

00:07:51.599 --> 00:07:55.949
She would personally visit every store that
carried Spanx to make sure that her product

00:07:55.949 --> 00:07:57.900
was in the optimal part of the store.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:01.250
No one is going to buy your product if it’s
sitting on the bottom of the shelf at the

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:02.509
back of the store.

00:08:02.509 --> 00:08:06.410
You want to be near the entrance or the cash
register so everyone sees it!

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:11.509
Sara knew this and she wouldn’t accept anything
less than best possible placement for Spanx.

00:08:11.509 --> 00:08:16.610
She was so bold in fact, that she once got
caught on security cameras personally moving

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:20.669
her product to a better section of the store
without telling management.

00:08:20.669 --> 00:08:23.539
But sometimes that’s what it takes to get
ahead of the competition.

00:08:23.539 --> 00:08:28.280
This is a big part of sales that often gets
overlooked, and it comes back to human psychology

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:29.280
again.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.610
Convincing someone to buy your product for
their store is just the first step in actually

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:34.130
growing your business.

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:38.659
Plenty of people can be persuaded in a one-on-one
conversation, only to lose interest the next

00:08:38.659 --> 00:08:39.659
day.

00:08:39.659 --> 00:08:43.530
Great entrepreneurs know that, even after
they win a customer, they have to work extra

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:47.500
hard to keep that customer happy and make
sure the relationship doesn’t fizzle out.

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:53.279
Sara’s hard work paid off and Spanx started
flying off shelves and then she got a huge

00:08:53.279 --> 00:08:54.279
break.

00:08:54.279 --> 00:08:58.010
She was invited on Oprah and got to pitch
Spanx to millions of viewers.

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:02.670
She told the perfect story around her product
and it resonated with millions of consumers.

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:15.870
Major celebrities started using Spanx and
endorsing it publicly:

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:20.730
With so much buzz, Sara clearly could have
raised boatloads of cash from top tier investors

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:26.290
with ease, but she didn’t, and that brings
me to Sara’s third key success factor: Sara

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:28.480
puts profitability first.

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:33.100
At every stage of Spanx growth, Sara has been
willing to get out of her comfort zone and

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:37.839
do things herself instead of paying others
and sacrificing profitability.

00:09:37.839 --> 00:09:42.390
Instead of hiring a fashion design firm, she
designed the first version of Spanx herself.

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:46.920
Instead of paying a law firm to file her patent,
she went to the library and wrote it herself.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:50.970
Instead of hiring sales people, she called
department stores herself.

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:55.269
Only when Spanx was making plenty of money
and could afford to hire people did she actually

00:09:55.269 --> 00:09:56.350
expand her team.

00:09:56.350 --> 00:10:00.250
But ultimately, it doesn’t matter how much
you’re willing to do yourself if your product

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:01.620
isn’t making enough money.

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:05.600
You need to make sure that the price your
customers pay for your product is significantly

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:07.670
higher than what it costs you to make it.

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:12.070
Many startup founders try not to think about
profitability early on, but Sara made sure

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:15.040
that each pair of Spanx made plenty of profit
for the business.

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:20.700
“A really good rule of thumb is five times
manufacturing cost, with packaging included.”

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:26.440
Good margins like that will give you the opportunity
to grow profitably without raising money,

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:28.070
and that’s exactly what Sara did.

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:33.120
She still owns 100% of Spanx and even though
the company hasn’t gone public, she’s

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:34.120
a billionaire.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:37.480
But there’s one more important thing to
note about her model of profitable growth.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:40.670
It’s really only an option if you commit
to it fully.

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:44.120
Once you raise money for your startup, your
investors are going to expect you to pursue

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:49.560
hypergrowth at all costs, and that often means
raising more money and eventually going public.

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:52.950
Some businesses are better suited for venture
capital funding than profitable growth from

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:56.350
day one, so really think about which model
makes sense for your business.

00:10:56.350 --> 00:10:58.910
It can be hard to change horses midstream.

00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:02.420
If you want to chat about startups, you can
always DM me on Twitter, and please check

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:05.730
out this recommended video, the YouTube algorithm
thinks you’ll really like it.

